.TH XPROXY\-ADMIN 8 "" "Xproxy" "System administration"
.nh
.ad l
.SH "NAME"
.PP
xproxy\-admin \- web interface for xproxy, in a process of its own
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.PP
\fBxproxy\-admin serve\fR [\fB\-listen\fR \fIADDR\fR] [\fB\-socket\fR \fIPATH\fR] [\fB\-config\fR \fIPATH\fR] [\fB\-users\fR \fIPATH\fR] [\fB\-tls\-cert\fR \fIPATH\fR \fB\-tls\-key\fR \fIPATH\fR [\fB\-client\-ca\fR \fIPATH\fR]] [\fB\-restart\-cmd\fR \fICOMMAND\fR] [\fB\-validate\fR]
.PP
\fBxproxy\-admin user add\fR \fINAME\fR [\fB\-role\fR viewer|operator] [\fB\-cert\-only\fR]
.PP
\fBxproxy\-admin user del\fR \fINAME\fR
.PP
\fBxproxy\-admin user list\fR
.PP
\fBxproxy\-admin passwd\fR \fINAME\fR
.PP
\fBxproxy\-admin version\fR
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
\fBxproxy\-admin\fR serves the browser interface for \fBxproxy\fR(8) and its siblings. It is a separate program and a separate process, and that is the point: the data plane terminates TLS for an estate and has no business also parsing session cookies, rendering HTML and writing configuration files. Everything this process decides it decides for itself; everything it \fIdoes\fR it asks the management socket to do, which is the same socket \fBxproxyctl\fR(8) uses and the same authorisation.
.PP
It holds no copy of the proxy\(aqs state. Each page is a question put to the management socket and the answer rendered, so a GUI that is out of date is a GUI that cannot reach the socket, which it says rather than showing a stale number.
.PP
\fBWhat it can change\fR is what the socket exposes: the ban list, the WAF\(aqs mode and rule statistics, rate\-limit and quota state, listener drain and maintenance, live sessions (list and kill), MFA enrolments, API keys, work orders against OT devices, the behaviour packs, and \[u2014] with \fB\-config\fR \[u2014] the configuration file itself, through the same dry\-run, diff and rollback path \fBxproxyctl policy\fR uses. A change is written, validated by the data plane, and either applied or refused with the reason.
.SS "Where it may listen"
.PP
A plaintext listener is permitted on the loopback address and a Unix socket, and nowhere else: any other address requires \fB\-tls\-cert\fR, \fB\-tls\-key\fR \fBand\fR \fB\-client\-ca\fR, and the process refuses to start otherwise. This is not advice in the documentation, it is a check in the code, because the alternative is an interface that can restart the estate\(aqs proxy reachable over the office network behind a password. The shipped unit binds \fB127.0.0.1:8443\fR and expects an SSH tunnel:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
.ft CR
ssh \-L 8443:127.0.0.1:8443 proxyhost
.ft
.fi
.RE
.SS "Who may log in"
.PP
Two roles. A \fBviewer\fR reads every page and changes nothing. An \fBoperator\fR may act: everything in \fIWhat it can change\fR above. The roles live in the users file, one \fBname:role:hash\fR line each, re\-read whenever it changes on disk \[u2014] so removing a user or downgrading a role takes effect without a restart, because revocation that needs a restart is revocation that silently does not happen.
.PP
Three ways to authenticate, which compose:
.RS 3n
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
A \fBpassword\fR from the users file (PBKDF2\-HMAC\-SHA256). Failed logins are rate limited per account.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
A \fBclient certificate\fR, when \fB\-client\-ca\fR is set: the common name is the user name. \fBuser add \-cert\-only\fR creates a user with no password at all, which is the arrangement worth having \[u2014] there is then no password to guess.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBSingle sign\-on\fR, when \fB\-oidc\-issuer\fR is set: the role comes from a claim (\fB\-oidc\-role\-claim\fR, default \fBgroups\fR) matched against \fB\-oidc\-operators\fR and \fB\-oidc\-viewers\fR.
.RE
.PP
.PP
Sessions are bounded twice, by idle time (\fB\-session\-idle\fR) and absolutely (\fB\-session\-max\fR), because a browser tab left open overnight is not a reason to stay logged in.
